      "writtenOn": "2026-11-01T04:49:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Would you mind keeping `security_vm_enough_memory_mm()` as upstream wrote it? Moving the LSM loop into a new helper also drops upstream\u0027s comment on how the modules vote on `cap_sys_admin`, and `security/security.c` changes a lot upstream.\n\nI think the conversion could live in `__vm_enough_memory()` instead, with `pages` still counting pages of `mm`:\n\n```\n\tpages \u003d vm_commit_mm_pages(mm, pages);\n\tvm_acct_memory_units(pages);\n```\n\nThen `security/security.c` and `include/linux/security.h` don\u0027t change at all, and `security_vm_enough_memory_native()` can go. The shmem and swapoff callers count kernel pages, so they would pass pages of `current-\u003emm` through a helper next to `mm_kernel_pages()`, e.g.:\n\n```\nstatic inline unsigned long mm_pages_from_kernel(const struct mm_struct *mm,\n\t\t\t\t\t\t unsigned long nr)\n{\n\treturn nr * mm_slices_per_page(mm);\n}\n```\n\nThe hooks would then see 4KB pages for shmem in 4KB processes, but `cap_vm_enough_memory()` and `selinux_vm_enough_memory()` ignore `pages`. Would that work for you?",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "f79c7e87fd32f53fac257c9e41d0356074b9a82d",
